WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 19 Leader Video
Unit 5, Lesson 3: Diety

1
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:11.180
So when you're talking to your students about Jesus, do you automatically assume

2
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.400
that they believe Jesus to be God?

3
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:22.120
For years I did. I just assumed it until I got into a conversation

4
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.660
with them about it. And I realized that that's not always the case.

5
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.100
Not that long ago, I was actually talking with a couple of young guys that came to

6
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:37.300
my door. They were Mormons. Super nice, very friendly, had a great conversation with

7
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:42.480
them. And naturally, I went right to talking about Jesus.

8
00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.460
Because at first we agreed on a ton of things, which I expected to be the case.

9
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.360
But then once we started talking about Jesus, although it seemed like we were going

10
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.340
to agree, it was very quickly realized that right where I thought we would disagree

11
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.740
is exactly where I thought we would disagree.

12
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.000
And I went right to John chapter 8.

13
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:11.380
And what I love about this passage is that Jesus just sort of flat out says it.

14
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:17.040
He just says who he is, which is partially why or almost entirely why they want the

15
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.760
Pharisees and the religious leaders want to kill him.

16
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.640
Because Jesus says before Abraham was born, I am.

17
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.260
And I asked these guys, what do you think Jesus means here?

18
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:32.300
What do you think this is? And they said, well, they stumbled from it.

19
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.120
They really couldn't come up with a good answer.

20
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.860
And they flipped to another passage and started talking about something different.

21
00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:42.620
And I tried to redirect them back and it reminded me of a lot of the conversations

22
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.500
that I've had with students over the years.

23
00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:50.560
And I, you know, making that mistake of just assuming that when we talk about Jesus

24
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.940
we're talking about the same thing.

25
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.920
So it's very possible that even in your context when you talk about Jesus you may be

26
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.240
talking on a different plane than some of your students are.

27
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:07.340
But this is a great opportunity to be crystal clear on exactly who it is that

28
00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.440
Jesus actually is. Who does he say he is, right?

29
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.000
And that's why we have students focus right in on John chapter 8 because it's here

30
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.760
that Jesus makes the flat-out statement, before Abraham was born, I am.

31
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.440
The focus here is who Jesus claims to be.

32
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.700
So we ask students in the intro activity to put down who they think they are,

33
00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.120
what their family and friends would say about them, and does that match up?

34
00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:33.600
And do they have evidence for this?

35
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.160
Like, they think they're somebody.

36
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.980
They think they're a soccer player or a Christian or a musician or a football player

37
00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:40.560
or whatever.

38
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.380
Do they have evidence for it? I used to call myself a football player because I

39
00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.440
played football, but a person once brought it up to me and said, you don't really

40
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.920
play football. I said, well, what do you mean?

41
00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:51.820
He said, well, you sit the bench.

42
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.660
So was I really a football player?

43
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.880
Probably not so much, right? So who do they think they are and what evidence for

44
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:05.780
it. So the trick of it is, is that if Jesus says who he is, but we have

45
00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:10.000
evidence for who he says he is, now we've got something, right?

46
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.740
If someone says they're a basketball player and they scored 35 points the night

47
00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.280
before, they're clearly a basketball player.

48
00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:22.340
There's evidence for it. So this is the time you could actually have your students

49
00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:27.720
do some of this interaction nicely and gently and with respect, of course, with

50
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.180
each other in your large group time to kind of get this idea of do I have evidence

51
00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:36.660
for who I think I am? And do I have evidence for who somebody else thinks that they

52
00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.380
are? So it'd be a good opportunity to do some of that interaction.

53
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.340
So in your small group time, one thing that I bet is probably going to come out and

54
00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:49.160
you'll want to make sure your small group leaders are ready to deal with it is it's

55
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:54.780
not hard, I don't think, to look at Jesus as a

56
00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:00.280
great moral teacher. As someone who has this wonderful religious sage

57
00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:05.940
kind of vibe going on where they can read what he says about love and forgiveness

58
00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.660
and acceptance and mercy and justice and all these great things that Jesus talks

59
00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.620
about like in the Sermon on the Mount kind of stuff and leave it there.

60
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.860
And then go on and say, I'm going to try to follow what Jesus says as a good

61
00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:26.420
moral teacher, but I'm not ready to accept him as the God of the

62
00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:31.940
universe, the creator of all. Because if I accept him as the God of the universe and

63
00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:36.840
the creator of all, that has wider implications for me than I'm ready to accept.

64
00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:42.700
See, it's easy to accept him as a good moral teacher because I can choose to ignore

65
00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:44.420
his teachings if I so choose to.

66
00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.780
I can do whatever I want when I want, and when I choose to follow his teachings, I

67
00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:53.000
look awesome. If I don't, no one's really going to notice, right?

68
00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.240
But if I'm held accountable to the God of the universe, that's a whole other story.

69
00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:04.960
So believe it or not, one thing that I found amongst some of my students back when I

70
00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.920
was teaching was that a lot of students really loved Jesus

71
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.900
and really liked what he taught, but had very little

72
00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.220
to no intention of ever following him as God.

73
00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.620
And that bothered me. And it was right there.

74
00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.760
It was under my nose and half the time I don't even know if I even noticed it.

75
00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:30.880
But after a while it became very apparent when I started looking for it.

76
00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.180
So it's changed the way I taught and it changed the way I started to converse with

77
00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.160
some of my students and I started to challenge them.

78
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.880
I think small group in this time, in this lesson might be a perfect opportunity for

79
00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:47.520
you to challenge your students in this question of how do you see Jesus?

80
00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.780
Similar to how Jesus addressed Peter in Matthew chapter 16.

81
00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:57.800
Who do you say I am? Ask your students, who do you say Jesus is?

82
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:03.040
And when they answer you, whatever their answer is, ask them why.

83
00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.920
Get some answers out of them as to why they think that.

84
00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:12.760
And if they're in that place where Jesus is just a good moral teacher, ask them what

85
00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.880
it would take to get them to see him as God.

86
00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.460
Is there enough evidence? I mean, you've presented the evidence.

87
00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.360
There's a whole bunch of it. If you look through all the verses, We've only given

88
00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.340
you a spattering of the verses that show that Jesus is God.

89
00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.960
So what does that mean for them?

90
00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.040
Is there not enough evidence, or are they just not willing?

91
00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.100
It can be an eye-opening experience.

92
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.860
I'll be honest. I'm not telling you if there's anything you can actually do about it

93
00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.040
physically right at this moment,

94
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:42.480
but it definitely will change the way you approach those students, the way you

95
00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:45.000
address them, the way you teach them, and the way you pray for them.

96
00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.540
So I would encourage you to dive into that.

97
00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.280
It's sort of a scary place to dive into a little bit, but I would encourage you to

98
00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:50.620
do that.

